    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i.jpg" alt="James VI and I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JamesIEngland_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to John de Critz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Great Britain was formed by the union of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The two had shared a monarch since 1603, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> as James I of England, but they kept separate parliaments, churches, laws and trade, and a union imposed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> had lapsed at the Restoration. Union became urgent after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">English Act of Settlement</a> of 1701 fixed the succession on the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a>, while the Scottish Parliament’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-security-1704">Act of Security</a> of 1704 reserved the right to choose a different successor. England replied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alien-act-1705">Alien Act</a> of 1705, threatening to treat Scots as foreigners and ban Scottish imports unless Scotland negotiated. Commissioners from both kingdoms agreed a treaty in 1706, and the Scottish Parliament ratified it in January 1707 amid popular protest, persuaded by access to English and colonial markets, by the ‘Equivalent’, a payment that partly compensated investors in the failed Darien scheme, and by guarantees for the Presbyterian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> and Scots law. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707">Acts of Union</a> took effect on 1 May 1707, when Queen Anne became the first monarch of Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Scotland sent 45 members to the House of Commons and 16 elected peers to the House of Lords of the new Parliament at Westminster.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/london"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="London" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Skyline_(125508655).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ilya Grigorik, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Great Britain was a commercial and increasingly urban society led by a powerful landed aristocracy. Land remained the main source of wealth and political influence, while the enclosure of open fields, new crop rotations and selective breeding raised agricultural output. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, with over half a million people in 1700 and nearly a million by 1801, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s largest city and the centre of trade, finance and print. Overseas commerce grew rapidly under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navigation-acts">Navigation Acts</a>, which reserved colonial trade for British ships: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and Barbados, tobacco from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and textiles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were re-exported across Europe, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> prospered on tobacco after the union. Ships from London, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, carrying more than two million Africans to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> during the century. From the 1760s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> began, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-hargreaves">James Hargreaves</a>’s spinning jenny, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-arkwright">Richard Arkwright</a>’s water frame and factories, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-watt">James Watt</a>’s improved steam engine, patented in 1769, and canals such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridgewater-canal">Bridgewater Canal</a> of 1761. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> were established churches, while Protestant Dissenters and Catholics faced legal restrictions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee</a> houses, newspapers and periodicals such as <em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-spectator-1711">Spectator</a></em> fed a lively public sphere of debate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies.jpg" alt="Battle of Ramillies" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies">Battle of Ramillies</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_slag_bij_Ramillies_tussen_de_Fransen_en_de_Geallieerden,_23_mei_1706,_SK-A-4061.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rijksmuseum, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>British power rested above all on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, the largest in the world for most of the century, financed by excise taxes and a public debt managed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a>. The army was small by continental standards, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> fought its European wars largely through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> to allies and hired German troops. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">John Churchill</a>, Duke of Marlborough, won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies">Ramillies</a> in 1706, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-oudenarde">Oudenarde</a> in 1708 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-malplaquet">Malplaquet</a> in 1709, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> (1713) gave Britain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Minorca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asiento">asiento</a></em> to supply enslaved Africans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>. Jacobite risings in support of the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">Stuarts</a> were defeated in 1715 and in 1745–1746, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> before his army was destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden">Battle of Culloden</a>. The Seven Years’ War (1756–1763) brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>’s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757, the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">James Wolfe</a>’s victory on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-plains-of-abraham">Plains of Abraham</a> in 1759 and naval victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-quiberon-bay">Quiberon Bay</a>. The American War of Independence ended in defeat after the surrender at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-yorktown">Yorktown</a> in 1781, but from 1793 Britain led coalitions against revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> destroyed a French fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-nile">Battle of the Nile</a> in 1798.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701.jpg" alt="Act of Settlement 1701" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement 1701</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_England_(1694%E2%80%931702).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sodacan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Great Britain was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> in which Parliament, made supreme by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Revolution of 1688</a>, controlled taxation and the standing army. On Queen Anne’s death in 1714 the crown passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement</a> to George I, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, and the Hanoverian kings, though still powerful in choosing ministers and directing foreign policy, needed majorities in the Commons. Cabinet government developed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Robert Walpole</a>, First Lord of the Treasury from 1721 to 1742 and usually regarded as the first prime minister, who managed Parliament through patronage. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septennial-act-1716">Septennial Act</a> of 1716 lengthened parliaments to seven years. Elections were dominated by landowners: the electorate was small, and many boroughs, the so-called rotten and pocket boroughs, were controlled by patrons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whigs-british-political-party">Whigs</a> dominated government until the 1760s, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> sought a more active role; long ministries followed under Lord North from 1770 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a> from 1783. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> kept its own courts, law and church, and its administration was largely left to political managers such as the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-dundas">Henry Dundas</a>. Local government lay with justices of the peace drawn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, while an efficient excise service gave the state a fiscal reach that few rivals could match.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states.jpg" alt="Constitution of the United States" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constitution_of_the_United_States_-_DPLA_-_9ca804144bd5965e992ae3528bc3c6a3_(page_1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Constitutional Convention, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Eighteenth-century Great Britain created the conditions for its later world dominance. The ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiscal-military-state">fiscal-military state</a>’, combining parliamentary taxation, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">funded national debt</a> and a large navy, allowed a country with far fewer people than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to defeat it repeatedly, and the victories of the Seven Years’ War left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the leading colonial power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, a territorial ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> after 1765. Its constitution, praised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> in <em>The Spirit of the Laws</em> (1748), influenced European reformers and the framers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a> flourished after the union: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>, whose <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wealth-of-nations">Wealth of Nations</a></em> appeared in 1776, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-ferguson">Adam Ferguson</a> shaped modern philosophy, economics and social science, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-johnson">Samuel Johnson</a>’s <em>Dictionary</em> (1755) and the novels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-defoe">Daniel Defoe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-richardson">Samuel Richardson</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-fielding">Henry Fielding</a> helped establish modern English prose and the novel. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> that began in these decades would transform the world economy. The thirteen colonies became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the settlement of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> from 1788 began British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. British ships were also the largest carriers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, against which an abolition movement, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-clarkson">Thomas Clarkson</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a>, organised from 1787.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783.jpg" alt="Treaty of Paris (1783)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris (1783)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Paris_(1783).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">iDK, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Great Britain did not decline so much as merge into a larger state. The loss of the thirteen American colonies, recognised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> in 1783, was a severe blow, but trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> recovered quickly and the empire shifted its weight towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and the Pacific. The decisive change came from Ireland, a separate kingdom under the British crown whose Parliament had won legislative independence in 1782. Inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">Society of United Irishmen</a> led a rebellion in 1798, supported by a small French landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-mayo">County Mayo</a>; it was suppressed with heavy loss of life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a> concluded that only a full union would secure Ireland and close a back door to French invasion, and he hoped to accompany it with Catholic emancipation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707">Acts of Union</a> of 1800, passed by the British and Irish parliaments after extensive use of patronage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a>. On 1 January 1801 Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a>, with Irish members sitting at Westminster. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> refused to accept Catholic emancipation, and Pitt resigned in 1801. Great Britain remains the name of the island and of the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> at the core of today’s United Kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Great Britain, created by the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1707, combined parliamentary government, public credit and naval power to defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> repeatedly, built a commercial empire across the Atlantic and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and was the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> and home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a> before its merger with Ireland in 1801.</em></p>

